## Lingua: shared vocabulary, not a global ruler

A **Lingua** is an identifiable collection of Concepts an Organ uses
privately, shares with peers, publishes, or adopts from elsewhere — common
ground between ontologies, not a required global schema.

```text
lingua
    uid, name, owner Organ?
    visibility          private | shared | public
    Concepts            adopted by membership
```

- [x] Adopting a foreign Concept preserves its uid and lineage; re-adoption is
  a no-op. Unknown precise Concepts may fall back to the nearest known
  ancestor while the original is retained.
- [x] Usage spans: a private personal Lingua, a small shared Lingua between
  peers, Institute-published defaults (non-ruling), an adopted/mapped famous
  external vocabulary, or genuinely unknown meaning left unknown rather than
  falsely normalized.
- [x] Concepts needed to interpret synchronized assertions travel with the
  data. Sync currently carries ordinary assertions when subject and (if
  present) object are both included; identity travels with the Record seed.

## What is left

### Lingua — what is left

#### Lince's own documentation, as Records

- [ ] **One Organ mirrors `docs/records/` in `lingua`.** The folder exists and
  is proven adoptable; what is left is a runtime setting in the Organ sand,
  which is the owner's to make. **The four Concepts have to exist in that Cell
  FIRST** — `idea`, `chapter`, `position`, `see-also`, created in the Ontology
  sand — or every one of the 44 files is refused, because a file must never
  invent a meaning. The test creates them itself, so green there does NOT mean
  a fresh Cell will take the folder. Whether the Instinct bundle should
  carry its own vocabulary is the honest fix, and it belongs with the build
  step below rather than here. **Never point File Sync at `docs/` itself** — the disk half runs FIRST
  and turns every file of the configured extension into a Record, so a folder
  aimed at `docs/` would adopt `Ontology.md` and the rest as Records the moment
  the format included markdown, deletions included. Only the configured
  extension is read today, which is what makes `.lingua` safe beside the
  existing `.md` files, but that is a property of the setting and not of the
  folder. Note the direction inverts once, and only once: everywhere else the
  Records are the authority and `docs/` is their projection, but the first
  files here are written by hand and adopted.

- [ ] **The prose of `docs/` joins the same Record set.** The parts of
  `Ontology.md`, `Karma.md`, `Transfer.md` and `Interface.md` that describe
  features already built are documentation living in the wrong file. Distil
  them into the Instinct Records so the two cannot differ — the same claim
  written twice is the problem this whole group exists to end. Requires
  judgement, unlike the chapter conversion: prose here explains a decision to
  whoever is building, and Instinct explains a feature to whoever is using,
  and not every paragraph survives that change of audience.

**A checkbox does NOT have to become its own Record** — dropped 2026-08-18.
This group used to require it: every box a task Record, and a file holding a
checkbox was drift by definition. That rule cost more than it bought. The
development of Ontology has been done against its implementation clusters, C0
through C11, and the value of a cluster is that you read a whole one at once —
what is built, what moved, what is left, in the order it has to happen.
Exploding that into a hundred task Records loses the reading and gains only
uniformity. So a `.lingua` Record may hold its own checkbox list in its body,
and `Ontology` holds the cluster list itself.

What still holds is the part that was actually about drift: there is ONE place
a given task is written. A box in `Ontology`'s cluster list and a box in a
branch Record saying the same thing is the same claim written twice, and that
is what this group exists to end — not the checkbox itself.

- [ ] **`docs/` ends with NO Markdown in it at all** — only `.lingua`. The
  owner's end state, 2026-08-16, and the reason the distillation above has to
  be complete rather than partial: a half-converted `docs/` is the worst of
  both, two sources that each look authoritative. Markdown stays a supported
  File Sync format, and keeps its precedence rules; it simply stops being how
  this project's own documentation is written. Nothing is deleted until its
  content is provably inside a Record — the losslessness check is what
  licenses the removal, which is why it sits above this and not below.

- [ ] **A mention in a body becomes a link.** `[[Title|uid]]` inside body text,
  the first mention of each other Record per file, later mentions left plain.
  Two rules, both about the op log: the **uid is authoritative and the title is
  decoration**, and renaming a Record must NOT rewrite the bodies that mention
  it — a rename that rewrote bodies would be an op storm across every peer for
  a cosmetic change. "Once per file, first mention" is a rule for whoever
  generates the text, never for the parser.

- [ ] **A build step turns `docs/records/` into an Instinct Trail.** It reads
  the Lingua folder and emits the bundle the sand ships. Ordering comes from
  the assertions.

- [ ] **Instinct ships Records, not pages**, with an explicit "put these in my
  Lince" that imports the bundle as a Trail. Importing is a normal consented
  import, so the same view says what it is about to add before it adds it. The
  chapters keep working as reading material either way; what changes is that
  the reader can take the subject matter into their own store, which is what
  lets a later Karma chapter populate Karma and let them watch it run.

- [ ] **Full CRUD over Instinct Records once imported**, including replacing
  the body of a shipped one. A built-in Record you cannot edit is a page with
  extra steps.

- [ ] Revoke — the honest part: revoking stops all future ops for that
  record, but the remote already holds what it saw, and a "delete your copy"
  instruction is *unenforceable* — the remote runs its own code and promised
  nothing. Spec it as two separate things: revoke (hard, local, guaranteed:
  nothing more travels) and `forget` request (a polite op the remote MAY
  honor; honoring is a trust signal, not a protocol guarantee). Never let UI
  imply revoke reaches into another Organ.
